WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 257: Henry Ford: The Quadricycle, the Model T, the Five-Dollar Day, and the Assembly Line

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.760
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non president American series explores the industrial colossus that reshaped the geography, workforce, and economy of the 20th century.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.000
[Rebecca] The farm boy who democratized mobility and put the world on wheels.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Henry Ford.

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:43.920
[George] Born in July 1863 on a prosperous family farm in Springwell's Township, Michigan, just outside the growing city of Detroit, Henry Ford grew up with a passionate, deep-seated hatred of the grueling, backbreaking manual drudgery of agricultural life.

7
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:53.760
[George] Watching horses and men struggle through mud to plow fields and harvest crops, young Henry became obsessed with machinery that could relieve human and animal suffering.

8
00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:06.880
[George] At age 12, while riding in a wagon with his father, Henry experienced an electrifying revelation when they encountered a horse-drawn portable nickels and shepherd's steam engine chugging down the road under its own power.

9
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.840
[George] Henry immediately jumped down from the wagon to interview the driver and inspect the gears.

10
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:22.880
[George] Blessed with natural mechanical dexterity, young Henry repaired pocket watches for neighbors using knitting needles and tweezers, learning the precise interaction of escapements and cogs.

11
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:32.080
[George] At age 16, against his father's wishes, Ford walked nine miles into Detroit to apprentice as a machinist in steam and rail workshops.

12
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:42.640
[George] Later becoming the chief engineer of the Edison Illuminating Company in Detroit, earning the personal encouragement of his idol Thomas Edison to pursue internal combustion engines.

13
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:52.720
[George] Young Henry's childhood resentment of agricultural toil fueled his lifelong mission to harness mechanical power to liberate working families from exhausting physical labor.

14
00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:05.640
[George] His mother's untimely death when he was twelve left a profound emotional scar, prompting Henry to vow that he would dedicate his life to building mechanical machines that reduced the physical burdens of domestic and farm labor.

15
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:16.360
[George] His apprenticeship in Detroit machine shops taught him the precision requirements of marine steam engines and industrial dynamos, preparing him for the internal combustion revolution.

16
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:31.080
[George] In the spring of 1896, working night after night in a drafty brick carriage shed behind his rented home on Bagley Avenue in Detroit, 32-year-old Henry Ford assembled his first experimental automobile, the quadricycle.

17
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:46.680
[George] Powered by a four-horsepower, two-cylinder gasoline engine fabricated from an old exhaust pipe, the vehicle was mounted on four 28-inch bicycle tires, steered with a boat tiller, and equipped with a leather belt transmission and a doorbell for a horn.

18
00:02:46.680 --> 00:03:01.800
[George] At two o'clock on the rainy morning of June 4, 1896, with his loyal wife Clara holding an umbrella and a kerosene lantern, Ford prepared to test the machine, only to realize in dismay that the vehicle was wider than the shed's doorway.

19
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] Grabbing an axe, Ford smashed out the brick wall, cranked the engine, and rolled out into the wet, dark streets of Detroit on a triumphant midnight test drive.

20
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.000
[George] The quadricycle proved his mechanical theories.

21
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:31.640
[George] Prompting Ford to launch two unsuccessful automotive ventures before gathering twelve forward thinking investors, including coal merchant Alexander Malcolmson and the Dodge brothers, to establish the Ford Motor Company in June 1903 with $8,000 in capital.

22
00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:42.760
[George] The midnight test drive of the quadricycle through the rain-slicked streets of Detroit proved that a lightweight, gasoline-powered vehicle could operate reliably without complex steam boilers.

23
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:52.560
[George] Clara Ford's unwavering faith in her husband's mechanical vision sustained him through nights of exhaustion, earning her his lifelong nickname, The Believer.

24
00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:03.840
[George] The founding of the Ford Motor Company in 1903 brought together visionary Detroit investors who recognized Ford's unyielding determination to build durable, functional motorcars.

25
00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:19.440
[George] In the early 1900s, the nascent American automobile industry was held hostage by the Association of Licensed Automobile Manufacturers, which claimed a legal monopoly over all gasoline-powered vehicles based on a broad patent held by George B.

26
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.560
[George] Selden, a New York patent lawyer who had never manufactured an operable car.

27
00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:34.720
[George] The trust demanded steep royalties from every manufacturer and ordered Ford to stop producing low-priced automobiles, arrogance that ignited Ford's combative temperament.

28
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:52.320
[George] Ford boldly defied the syndicate, advertising that he would protect every Ford buyer against patent lawsuits at his own expense, and engaging in an epic eight-year legal war that culminated in 1911, when a federal appeals court ruled that the Selden patent was invalid for modern engines.

29
00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.520
[George] Free from monopoly restrictions, Ford pursued his radical commercial gospel.

30
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.480
[George] Other manufacturers built expensive, ornate roadsters for the wealthy elite.

31
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:16.400
[George] Ford envisioned a car for the great multitude, a rugged, lightweight, affordable vehicle built of high-strength vanadium steel, simple enough for a farmer to repair with baling wire and cheap enough for working families to buy.

32
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:27.520
[George] Ford's courage in challenging the Selden Patent Trust broke the stranglehold of Wall Street financiers over the young automobile industry, defending the rights of independent manufacturers.

33
00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:38.600
[George] The landmark legal triumph over the Selden Trust established Henry Ford as an authentic folk hero of American competitive capitalism, championing the rights of everyday consumers.

34
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:50.040
[George] His commitment to vanadium steel produced an automobile chassis that was twice as strong as ordinary carbon steel yet significantly lighter, capable of withstanding rough frontier roads.

35
00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:58.360
[George] On October 1, 1908, the Ford Motor Company introduced the Model T, affectionately nicknamed the Tin Lizzy.

36
00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.640
[George] It was a masterpiece of utilitarian engineering.

37
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:15.400
[George] Its high ground clearance allowed it to navigate muddy country ruts where horses bogged down, its planetary transmission made it easy to operate, and its 20 horsepower engine could run on gasoline, kerosene or ethanol.

38
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:26.280
[George] Priced initially at $850, the Model T became an overnight sensation, generating thousands of backorders that overwhelmed Ford's Piquette Avenue plant.

39
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.960
[George] Demanding absolute standardization, Ford famously decreed to his sales managers.

40
00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] Any customer can have a car painted any color that he wants so long as it is black, a decision driven purely by practical efficiency, as Japan black enamel was the only paint that dried fast enough to match factory production speeds.

41
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.320
[George] The Model T broke the isolation of rural America.

42
00:06:49.320 --> 00:07:01.400
[George] It carried farmers to church, physicians to rural emergencies, and families on weekend picnics, sparking nationwide demands for paved highways and transforming the American landscape forever.

43
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:12.440
[George] The Model T's universal popularity transformed American culture, bridging the geographic divide between isolated farming communities and bustling urban industrial centers.

44
00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:24.640
[George] The Model T's universal ruggedness allowed it to be converted into tractor engines, grain grinders, and portable electric power units, proving to be the ultimate mechanical workhorse of the frontier.

45
00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:34.400
[George] The standardization on Japan black paint reflected Ford's relentless obsession with production efficiency, eliminating drying bottlenecks on the factory floor.

46
00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:46.080
[George] To meet the insatiable global demand for the Model T, Ford constructed a colossal 60-acre factory at Highland Park, Michigan, designed by industrial architect Albert Kahn.

47
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:55.520
[George] Inspired by the overhead conveyor systems used in Chicago meatpacking slaughterhouses and grain elevators, Ford and his production engineers, including C.

48
00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:55.840
[George] W.

49
00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:57.440
[George] Wills, Peter E.

50
00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:05.360
[George] Martin, and Charles Sorensen, introduced the world's first moving automobile assembly line in the autumn of 1913.

51
00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:14.880
[George] Instead of mechanics walking around a stationary chassis to assemble a car, the vehicle was pulled down a long ramp by a continuous motorized steel cable.

52
00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:23.280
[George] While workers stood at specialized stations performing single, repetitive tasks as parts were delivered to their hands by gravity chutes.

53
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.040
[George] The impact on industrial productivity was astronomical.

54
00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:34.160
[George] The time required to assemble a Model T chassis plummeted from 12 hours and eight minutes to just 93 minutes.

55
00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:48.080
[George] By 1914, Ford was producing more automobiles than all other manufacturers in the world combined, while the price of the Model T steadily dropped from $850 to an astonishing $260.

56
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:58.160
[George] The moving assembly line at Highland Park slashed the cost of automobile production, turning mass manufacturing into a rhythmic, synchronized industrial ballet.

57
00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:09.960
[George] Highland Park was hailed by foreign engineers as an industrial cathedral, whose continuous conveyor belts and specialized machine tools redefined the manufacturing philosophy of the modern era.

58
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:20.280
[George] By dropping the price of the Model T to under $300, Ford placed car ownership within reach of ordinary schoolteachers, farmers and factory workers.

59
00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:39.720
[George] On January 5, 1914, Henry Ford shocked the global business community by announcing a double revolutionary policy, cutting the standard factory workday from 9 hours to 8, and more than doubling the minimum wage for autoworkers from $2.34 a day to $5 a day.

60
00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:48.680
[George] Rival industrialists and Wall Street financiers condemned Ford as an irresponsible madman and socialist traitor, predicting instant bankruptcy.

61
00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:52.280
[George] But Ford's move was a stroke of visionary capitalism.

62
00:09:52.280 --> 00:10:06.520
[George] High wages virtually eliminated costly worker turnover, attracted the most skilled mechanics in America, and transformed his own factory workers into prosperous consumers who could afford to buy the very cars they assembled on the line.

63
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] The $5 day ignited an unprecedented mass migration to Detroit, as tens of thousands of workers, including European immigrants and African Americans from the rural South, flooded the city.

64
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:27.400
[George] Ford had inadvertently invented the modern consumer society and laid the economic foundation of the 20th century American middle class.

65
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:38.280
[George] The $5 day ignited an economic revolution, proving that paying workers living wages created the consumer demand that fueled industrial prosperity across the nation.

66
00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:50.000
[George] Wall Street commentators who predicted doom were silenced as Ford's annual profits soared, proving that treating labor as valued consumer partners was the cornerstone of enduring business success.

67
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:58.960
[George] The policy attracted thousands of ambitious workers to Detroit, transforming the motor city into the manufacturing capital of the global economy.

68
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:17.680
[George] In the 1920s, Ford constructed the River Rouge Complex in Dearborn, Michigan, the largest industrial manufacturing facility on Earth, spanning 2,000 acres with its own steel mills, glass furnaces, 90 miles of railroad tracks, and electric generating plants.

69
00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:24.400
[George] Capable of transforming raw iron ore from Minnesota into a finished automobile in just 33 hours.

70
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:34.560
[George] By the time the final Model T rolled off the line in May 1927, over 15 million had been manufactured, comprising half the automobiles on planet Earth.

71
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:44.000
[George] Yet Ford's legacy was deeply complex, an uncompromising pacifist who sailed a peace ship to Europe in 1915 to halt World War I.

72
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:53.280
[George] He also published anti-Semitic writings in his newspaper The Dearborn Independent, and violently resisted the unionization of his plants until 1941.

73
00:11:53.280 --> 00:12:04.480
[George] When Henry Ford died in April 1947 at age 83, over 100,000 mourners stood in lines for miles in the rain to file past his casket in Greenfield Village.

74
00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:15.280
[George] Henry Ford permanently altered the human condition, proving that mass production and fair wages could democratize luxury, conquer distance, and build an industrial nation.

75
00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:26.320
[George] The River Rouge Complex stood as the supreme architectural monument of industrial vertical integration, converting raw iron ore into finished automobiles on a colossal scale.

76
00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:38.840
[George] His beloved Greenfield Village Historic Museum reflected his nostalgic reverence for 19th-century rural American craft, preserving historic buildings and mechanical workshops for future generations.

77
00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:50.200
[George] Henry Ford's life reshaped the modern world, proving that mass production, affordable mobility, and high wages could create the most prosperous middle class in human history.

78
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.200
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

79
00:12:54.200 --> 00:13:01.400
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 258.

80
00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.040
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

